 ## Abstract
 
-We prove explicit global convergence bounds for exact Sinkhorn iterations by converting a uniform curvature estimate, measured in the current marginal, into a relative-entropy error bound. If the source satisfies a Poincaré inequality with constant $C_P$ and the bounded cost has source gradient bounded by $L$, the dual gap contracts in each full iteration by at most $C_PL^2/(C_PL^2+\lambda^2)$, for every bounded initial potential and every target probability measure. Neither convexity of the source support nor a density assumption on the target is required. The argument uses finite-dimensional convex conjugacy on measurable partitions; it does not invert an infinite-dimensional Hessian. Applying the same transfer to an existing curvature theorem of Chizat, Delalande and Vaškevičius gives a global contraction $C/(C+\lambda)$ in their bounded-density setting, improving their global quadratic dependence on regularisation to linear dependence. Finally, one fixed source with density $(2/5)|x|\log^2(e/|x|)$ has an explicit Poincaré constant and local contraction deficit asymptotic to $(8/5)\log(2)\lambda^2\log^2(1/\lambda)$. Thus no smaller polynomial exponent than two is valid under a Poincaré assumption alone. The literature already supplies reverse-entropy methods and, indirectly, qualitative polynomial convergence in a smooth compact subclass. Our claims concern the explicit bounds, their stated scope, the global bounded-density improvement, and the exponent obstruction.
+We prove explicit global convergence bounds for exact Sinkhorn iterations by converting a uniform curvature estimate, measured in the current marginal, into a relative-entropy error bound. If the source satisfies a Poincaré inequality with constant $C_P$ and the bounded cost has source gradient bounded by $L$, the dual gap contracts in each full iteration by at most $C_PL^2/(C_PL^2+\lambda^2)$, for every bounded initial potential and every target probability measure. Neither convexity of the source support nor a density assumption on the target is required. The argument uses finite-dimensional convex conjugacy on measurable partitions; it does not invert an infinite-dimensional Hessian. Applying the same transfer to an existing curvature theorem of Chizat, Delalande and Vaškevičius gives a global contraction $C/(C+\lambda)$ in their bounded-density setting, improving their global quadratic dependence on regularisation to linear dependence. One fixed Poincaré source has local contraction deficit asymptotic to $(8/5)\log(2)\lambda^2\log^2(1/\lambda)$, while a family with uniformly bounded Poincaré constants attains order $\lambda^2$. The polynomial exponent is therefore sharp. The literature already supplies reverse-entropy methods and, indirectly, qualitative polynomial convergence in overlapping smooth settings. Our claims concern the explicit bounds, their stated scope, the global bounded-density improvement, and the sharpness constructions.
 
